          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"rostand-cyrano-de-bergerac-fcb8f2f7f81a\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.95,\"analysis\":\"Roxane directly presents her affection for Cyrano as true. The idiom “je vous aime bien” is most naturally an avowal of fondness rather than a relational undertaking. Neither the initial “Oh !” nor the affectionate staging independently shows that the words function as a reflex-like emotional discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"French “aimer bien” can range from liking to warm affection and is weaker than an unqualified romantic declaration; this affects the kind or degree of feeling avowed, not its predominantly truth-conditional force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"The classified event is Roxane’s directly spoken “Oh ! je vous aime bien” addressed to Cyrano, not the later repeated “Oh ! je vous aime !” in the supplied context.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Roxane expresses warm affection toward Cyrano while preparing to leave and asking him to tell another man to write to her. In the wider dramatic situation, that other man is Christian, the object of her romantic love, so her words to Cyrano convey fondness and gratitude rather than a new romantic commitment. Her haste, “Oh !,” and affectionate gesture make the scene emotionally warm but do not establish independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev-local-1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“ROXANE: Oh ! je vous aime bien. Il faut que je m'en aille.”\",\"supports\":\"The declarative construction explicitly avows Roxane’s affection, strongly supporting T; “bien” qualifies it as fondness or liking.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev-local-2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Dites-lui qu'il m'écrive.”\",\"supports\":\"Roxane’s request concerning an absent male distinguishes her affection for Cyrano from the romantic attachment organizing the immediate conversation; it supplies no undertaking toward Cyrano.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev-local-3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The passage includes “Oh !,” hurried masking, and later a small kiss sent by hand.\",\"supports\":\"These details indicate warmth and animated delivery, but they do not independently establish reflex-like production of the matched words.\",\"confidence\":0.93},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev-background-1\",\"source\":\"background_knowledge\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"In the play’s established plot, Roxane is romantically attracted to Christian and uses Cyrano as an intermediary, while Cyrano secretly loves her.\",\"supports\":\"This dramatic configuration supports reading “je vous aime bien” to Cyrano as sincere familial or friendly fondness rather than a romantic pledge.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":true,\"familiarity\":\"extensive\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"contribution\":\"The wider plot identifies Christian as the man from whom Roxane wants a letter and clarifies the contrast between her romantic love for Christian and her affectionate regard for Cyrano.\"},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the utterance as an avowal of affection. The surrounding emotional warmth and dramatic irony require contextual explanation but introduce no core force beyond T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The exact matched occurrence at source offsets 77920–77932 is the first phrase “je vous aime” within “je vous aime bien”; the later unqualified repetition is contextual evidence rather than the classified occurrence.\"}"
        }
